  "live_records": {
    "q-102425": {
      "dom_id": "step-q102425",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Find the Laplace transform of $y(t),$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}-3y={t}^{2},\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=2.\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}-3y\\} & =L\\{{t}^{2}\\} \\\\ L\\{{y}^{″}\\}-2L\\{{y}^{′}\\}-3L\\{y\\} & =L\\{{t}^{2}\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's introduce $L\\{y\\}=Y(s).$\n\nTo calculate $L\\{{y}^{′}\\}$ and $L\\{{y}^{″}\\},$  we use formulas for the Laplace transforms of the derivatives and apply the initial conditions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}\\} & =sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s) \\\\ L\\{{y}^{″}\\} & ={s}^{2}Y(s)-sy(0)-{y}^{′}(0)={s}^{2}Y(s)-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have the following result:\n\n$$\nL\\{{t}^{2}\\}=\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can solve for $Y(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{″}\\}-2L\\{{y}^{′}\\}-3L\\{y\\} & =L\\{{t}^{2}\\} \\\\ ({s}^{2}Y(s)-2)-2sY(s)-3Y(s) & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}} \\\\ ({s}^{2}-2s-3)Y(s) & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+2 \\\\ (s-3)(s+1)Y(s) & =\\frac{2+2{s}^{3}}{{s}^{3}} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{2(1+{s}^{3})}{{s}^{3}(s-3)(s+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
