    "q-83343": {
      "dom_id": "step-q83343",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Find the Laplace transform of $y(t),$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0.\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the scalar multiplication property of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{″}\\} & =L\\{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\} \\\\ L\\{{y}^{″}\\} & =2L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's introduce $L\\{y\\}=Y(s).$ \n\nTo calculate $L\\{{y}^{″}\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a second derivative:\n\n$$\nL\\{{y}^{″}\\}={s}^{2}L\\{y\\}-sy(0)-{y}^{′}(0)={s}^{2}Y(s)-sy(0)-{y}^{′}(0)\n$$\n\nTaking into account the given initial conditions, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{″}\\}={s}^{2}Y(s)-s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have the following result:\n\n$$\nL\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{″}\\} & =2L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\} \\\\ {s}^{2}Y(s)-s & =\\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ {s}^{2}Y(s) & =\\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}+1}+s \\\\ {s}^{2}Y(s) & =\\frac{{s}^{3}+3s}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{{s}^{3}+3s}{{s}^{2}({s}^{2}+1)} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{{s}^{2}+3}{s({s}^{2}+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
